- `std::invalid_argument`: Too few/nonfinite values, invalid mu/alternative/confidence or zero/nonfinite standard error.

C++ exceptions are raised as `NativeError`.

See also: `HypothesisTestResult`.

# Arguments
- `x::Vector{Float64}`: At least two finite observations with positive representable sample variance.
- `mu::Float64`: Finite null mean, default zero, in observation units.
- `alternative::Symbol`: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater, relative to mu.
- `conf_level::Float64`: Finite confidence level strictly in (0,1), default .95.

# Returns
`HypothesisTestResult`: statistic=t, parameter1=df, estimate1=sample mean, p_value and mean confidence bounds; remaining scalars retain defaults. One-sided intervals have one infinite endpoint.
"""
t_test_one_sample(x) = _Rosetta.call(201, (x,))
t_test_one_sample(x, mu) = _Rosetta.call(201, (x, mu))
t_test_one_sample(x, mu, alternative) = _Rosetta.call(201, (x, mu, alternative))
t_test_one_sample(x, mu, alternative, conf_level) = _Rosetta.call(201, (x, mu, alternative, conf_level))

"""
    t_test_two_sample(x::Vector{Float64}, y::Vector{Float64}, equal_variance::Bool=false, alternative::Symbol=:TwoSided, conf_level::Float64=0.95) -> HypothesisTestResult

Independent two-sample Welch or pooled Student mean test, like R t.test().

Welch uses unequal sample variances and Satterthwaite approximate df; normality alone does not
make its reference exact. Pooled Student requires equal population variances and independent
normal observations for its exact reference. CI estimates mean(x)-mean(y). O(n_x+n_y) work,
inputs unchanged, no shared mutable state or omission.

**Example**

```cpp
const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_two_sample({1,2,3},{4,5,6});
std::print(\"{:.0f}\\n\",t.estimate1); // 2
```

**References**

- https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html

