- `std::invalid_argument`: Nonfinite input/mu, invalid alternative, no nonzero differences or zero rank variance.

C++ exceptions are raised as `NativeError`.

See also: `HypothesisTestResult`.

# Arguments
- `x::Vector{Float64}`: Finite values; at least one nonzero difference from mu must remain.
- `mu::Float64`: Finite symmetry centre, default zero.
- `alternative::Symbol`: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for the signed location shift.

# Returns
`HypothesisTestResult`: statistic=positive rank sum V, estimate1=mu (not an estimated pseudomedian), approximate p_value; no CI.
"""
wilcoxon_signed_rank_test(x) = _Rosetta.call(205, (x,))
wilcoxon_signed_rank_test(x, mu) = _Rosetta.call(205, (x, mu))
wilcoxon_signed_rank_test(x, mu, alternative) = _Rosetta.call(205, (x, mu, alternative))

"""
    wilcoxon_rank_sum_test(x::Vector{Float64}, y::Vector{Float64}, alternative::Symbol=:TwoSided) -> HypothesisTestResult

Tie/continuity-corrected Mann-Whitney U test using a normal approximation.

Pools ranks, giving average ranks to ties, and reports U for the first sample. Its identical-
distribution null requires independent exchangeable samples. A common-shape shift model
supports a location interpretation; equal medians alone is insufficient. U/(n_x*n_y) estimates
P(X>Y)+P(X=Y)/2, not a mean difference. Approximation is used at every size. O(N log N)
work/O(N) storage, inputs unchanged, no shared state.

**Example**

```cpp
const auto w=dm::stats::wilcoxon_rank_sum_test({1,2,3},{4,5,6});
std::print(\"{:.0f}\\n\",w.statistic); // 0
```

**References**

- https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/wilcox.test.html

# Throws

- `std::invalid_argument`: Empty/nonfinite samples, invalid alternative or zero rank variance (all pooled observations tied).

C++ exceptions are raised as `NativeError`.

See also: `HypothesisTestResult`.

# Arguments
- `x::Vector{Float64}`: Nonempty finite first sample.
- `y::Vector{Float64}`: Nonempty finite independent second sample.
- `alternative::Symbol`: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for first-versus-second stochastic location.

# Returns
`HypothesisTestResult`: statistic=U=rank_sum(x)-n_x(n_x+1)/2 and approximate p_value; no CI or location estimate.
"""
wilcoxon_rank_sum_test(x, y) = _Rosetta.call(206, (x, y))
wilcoxon_rank_sum_test(x, y, alternative) = _Rosetta.call(206, (x, y, alternative))

"""
    bs(lower::Float64, upper::Float64, knots::Vector{Float64}, degree::Integer=3, intercept::Bool=false) -> Bspline

Construct an owned B-spline basis for repeated evaluation with eval().
Free-function spelling of Bspline: construction fixes the knots and boundaries
without evaluating a design matrix or fitting a model. The returned value owns
its basis; the input knot vector may be modified or destroyed after this call.

**Example**

```cpp
const auto basis = dm::bs(0, 1, dm::seql(0, 1, 3));
const auto design = basis.eval(dm::c(0, 0.5, 1));
std::print(\"{} {}\\n\", design.rows(), design.cols()); // 3 4
```

# Throws

- `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid boundaries, knots or overflowing degree.

C++ exceptions are raised as `NativeError`.

See also: `Bspline`, `Bspline::eval()`.

# Arguments
- `lower::Float64`: Finite left boundary, strictly below upper.
- `upper::Float64`: Finite right boundary.
- `knots::Vector{Float64}`: Finite, strictly increasing breakpoints in [lower, upper]. Boundary entries are optional; an empty vector creates a basis without interior knots.
- `degree::Integer`: Polynomial degree, default 3 (cubic).
- `intercept::Bool`: Keep the first basis column, default false. Leave false with an intercept formula such as `y ~ .`; use a no-intercept formula when true.

# Returns
`Bspline`: Owned Bspline with the same degree, intercept policy and validation as its constructor. Call eval() on training and prediction coordinates.
